1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Tim Jakes and I am Scoutmaster with Troop 93 in Westchester, Pennsylvania.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This edition of Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Hey, everybody, this is Clark Green. It's summertime and so our next few podcasts feature on-court presentations

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
from our archives. And this week I'm excited to play an interview with one of my absolute favorite outdoor authors,

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Kevin Callan, who is called the Happy Camper.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Kevin is a Canadian author, canoeist and just a marvelous guy who's written a whole slew of books about camping

8
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and guidebooks to the different parks in Canada,

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
And I always enjoy getting a chance to talk with him.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
As always, this podcast is only possible because of the generosity of the folks who become ScoutmasterCGcom backers.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Go to ScoutmasterCGcom, click the support link at the top of the page.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
You'll find out how you can become a ScoutmasterCGcom backer.

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
We'll be back with our regular schedule of podcasts in a few weeks.

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Until then, let's get started, shall we?

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Today I'm talking with Canadian author and canoe enthusiast, Kevin Callan, who is also known as the Happy Camper,

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And we've seen some of his videos on the Scoutmaster blog before.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,000
How are you doing Kevin?

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
How are you doing Clark? I am happy, Are you happy?

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
I am ecstatic because I'm talking to you.

20
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Your book about Algonquin Provincial Park and canoe routes in Algonquin was really instrumental into us building a program where we bring our Scouts up that way.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Well, that's great. What do you think of Algonquin?

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
It's an amazingly different place.

23
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
I'm convinced you could go paddling in Algonquin and around there for 20 years and never see the same water twice.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Oh, definitely, There's lots of places to go. That's a nice thing about the park.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
I mean, I only live an hour away from it, so that's the nicest thing too is I can just head up and paddle.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
My favorite place is the Nipissing River, the northwest corner,

27
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And it's just one of those tranquil little trope rivers that's hard to get to.

28
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
It's got old growth forest with old growth pine hang. It's just a beautiful place

29
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Because it's like a transition zone.

30
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The neat thing about Algonquin is that the forest ecology that's around that area continues south through southern Ontario or southeastern Ontario,

31
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
then right into the main area. you know the United States,

32
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
And it's this sort of corridor that's a lot of wildlife travel.

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
They figured out the last few years they've tracked the wolves and everything else and moose that travel this corridor all the time.

34
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It's kind of neat that we actually have that.

35
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
And now let me get this right. Is it portaging or portaging?

36
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Well, it depends on where you live.

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
It doesn't matter how you say it: Everybody hates one.

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Bill Mason said it best when he said: people who actually like portaging is either a liar or a crazy.

39
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And I got to say, though, the only thing that I think is left, though, that keeps wilderness wild, is a long portage,

40
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Because, you think about it, if you go three miles with a canoe in your head and a pack on your shoulder and stuff,

41
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
what you're going to get at the end is going to be worth it.

42
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
I don't care how people get into the woods. to be quite honest, I'm not a purist,

43
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
But I know a lot of people that drive the ATVs and stuff,

44
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
And I'll go up to him. I said: look, you always get on my case that you go into this lake, and it's not what it was a few years back.

45
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The reason why is it's too easy for you to get in here now,

46
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
And as soon as it's easy to get in, then the wilderness concept is gone and the trout are gone,

47
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
And if you actually put a canoe on your back and portage for three miles, then what you're going to get is going to be worth it.

48
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
And I think they're not getting that. They all want everything easy for them

49
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
And the nice thing is if you meet someone at the end of the portage- a really long portage- if you actually do see someone,

50
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
they're going to be just like you because they actually went through the same thing that you just did.

51
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Well, and we should explain for the non canoe heads out there: a portage or a portage where you take all your gear,

52
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
But basically you're backpacking with a canoe.

53
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Yeah, In fact, my wife loves backpacking. We always do a backpacking trip every year,

54
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
But I always curse at her: I go, you know, this is the longest portage ever.

55
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
You know all you're doing is walking with a pack on.

56
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Yeah, There's no paddling involved at all. Come on.

57
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,000
No, what's with that?

58
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
There's a lot more canoeing in Ontario, isn't there?

59
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Oh, yeah, We've got so many bodies of water that really the canoe was invented for the landscape.

60
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Some say the landscape was invented for the canoe,

61
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
But it opened up Historically. it opened up Canada.

62
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
We should come back in Ontario.

63
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
And you know, Americans got the, I guess, the Chuck wagon and the cowboy hat, whatever. got to the west

64
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
We had the Voyager canoe,

65
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:27,000
So for a little bit of driving, if you're coming from New York State, Ohio, Pennsylvania or any of the New England states,

66
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
you can be in Canoe Paradise pretty quickly up over the line in Ontario.

67
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Well yeah, if you're going from that area, you've got not only a Gonkwin, you've got Tomoghame,

68
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:42,000
which is actually just an hour or maybe a couple hours north of Gonkwin.

69
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
It's a vast wilderness in endless rivers. The Sturgeon River is one of my favorite

70
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:55,000
which is, oh you know, for three days it's almost continual class three or class one, class two rapids

71
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And you won't see anybody.

72
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
You were talking a little bit about the Upper Ottawa.

73
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Yeah, the Upper Ottawa, It was kind of a miracle.

74
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
My wife- actually my wife- has strict rules when we go on her holiday canoe trips.

75
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
I'm not allowed to work and ride about them.

76
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
My daughter was turning six years old and she said:

77
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
I want to go on another canoe trip for seven days.

78
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
I want to go somewhere we haven't gone before, which is not that easy, because I go everywhere

79
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
and wanted to be an easy trip and not a lot of portage.

80
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Well, the Ottawa River. I never thought it would be the way it is,

81
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,000
But up from Mattawa, which is where the Champlain Park is. we started there

82
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
and we paddled down to what's called Driftwood Provincial Park,

83
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
which was halfway between Tomogamy and the city of Ottawa,

84
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
And you could do it in five days, but we did it in seven.

85
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And there's no rapids, It's just a strong current.

86
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
There's one town halfway through and then we got supplies there,

87
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
But we saw 12 motor boats and that's it in seven days.

88
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
And we saw one other canoe group

89
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And this is the Ottawa River,

90
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Like everybody in Canada knows, the Ottawa River.

91
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
So on one side you had the Laurentian Mountains

92
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and the other side you had the Ontario Bush,

93
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
And it was one of those things where you think you've seen it all

94
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and then you realize that a lot of people ask me

95
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
what's my favorite canoe trip,

96
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
And it's always. it sounds bizarre,

97
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
but my answer is the next one. I'm going on

98
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Because it really doesn't matter where you're going.

99
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
It matters that you go right.

100
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
I think you're absolutely right.

101
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
You just have to go.

102
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
There's just some marvelous opportunities for people

103
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
to take a group of scouts up there

104
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and not to spend a whole lot of money,

105
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,000
And you can have a marvelous trip.

106
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
I guess you could probably throw a dart at a map of Ontario

107
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and just go just about anywhere, couldn't you?

108
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Oh yeah,

109
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Then that's. the nice thing about it too

110
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
is that there's so many other places

111
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
outside of parks you can paddle,

112
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
Even in Northwestern Ontario. a couple of years ago

113
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
we went down what's called the Cupca River

114
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and it's south of Wabakimi.

115
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Now you can get to Wabakimi just by train

116
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
From the Toronto train station, for everything,

117
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
But they drop you off.

118
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
But we went down the Cupca

119
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and it was one of those rivers that not many people knew.

120
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
In fact it was. Cliff Jegas, in an American nation,

121
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
told me about it

122
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
And we went down that river

123
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
and we paddled to Nipagon, the North Shore, Nipagon,

124
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,000
which is an oasis.

125
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,000
It's just so beautiful.

126
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
It's one of the inland sort of great lakes

127
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
And we spent five weeks paddling

128
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and we weren't in the far north,

129
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,000
We didn't spend a fortune.

130
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
In fact it didn't cost as much at all to get to do that river.

131
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
So there's lots of places to go

132
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
and it's pretty cheap.

133
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
And any place that there's good paddling,

134
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
usually there's going to be an outfitter nearby-

135
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Yeah, there usually is,

136
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
And there's a lot of information nowadays

137
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
on the computer obviously

138
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
to where to get the information

139
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
and if it's a good outfitter and that sort of thing.

140
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
But most likely in areas like really well used areas

141
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
like the Gonquin Tomography,

142
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
even Wabakimi or Quiddicoke,

143
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
there's at least a half a dozen outfitters

144
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
that are going to help you out.

145
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
And I don't own a canoe.

146
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
I do have a lovely paddle,

147
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
which is kind of like having a leash without a puppy.

148
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Sometimes I think a paddle is more important to you

149
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
than the canoe.

150
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
You think about it.

151
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
You paddle 10,000 strokes a day on a good paddle day.

152
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
So, yeah, I'd sleep with my paddle if I were you.

153
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
It is really pretty simple

154
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
to get in with an outfitter

155
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
and to find your way up there.

156
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
You know you're working on a book right now

157
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
about some of the places to go canoeing in Ontario.

158
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Yeah, what I did is I wrote eight guide books

159
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
throughout my years on where to go paddling

160
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and come back in Ontario

161
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And then my publisher said:

162
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
can you do the best of like the Beatles White Album

163
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
or whatever it's called,

164
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,000
I don't know.

165
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
But I said, yeah, I can do that,

166
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
but I'd like to add new ones as well.

167
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
And what they did is that

168
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
I chose 40 of my favorite roots in Ontario

169
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
and then 10 new ones

170
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And that'll be out in March

171
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
And it's called Terrell's Best Canoe Roots.

172
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Yeah,

173
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
But there's some new ones, though

174
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
that really might interest you

175
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
because it's not really being written about before.

176
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
And there's an old canoe route

177
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
in the south end of Tomoghani.

178
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
There's a place called Chinakuchi.

179
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
Now there's a place near Chinakuchi.

180
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
This is just north of a gulking park

181
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
that is called Kalarni Park.

182
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Now that is the most scenic place

183
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,000
in Ontario, Kalarni Park.

184
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
It's got quartzite mountains,

185
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
It's got turquoise water.

186
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
But there's a place near there,

187
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
near Sudbury, called Chinakuchi,

188
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
And it's a south access to Tomoghani.

189
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
But what I did is I went up to Chinakuchi.

190
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Now they created that into a new park back in 1999

191
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
but they haven't done much to it,

192
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
So there's no reservation period.

193
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Basically nobody really goes there.

194
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And what I did is I went up through Chinakuchi River

195
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
but then I hooked up into Tomoghani.

196
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
So I went through a bunch of Tomoghani lakes

197
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
and then I hooked up to the Sturgeon River

198
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and I went down the Sturgeon River back to Chinakuchi

199
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
And it's six, maybe eight days,

200
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
But it's one of the best canoe trips I can ever imagine,

201
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
because you've got a little bit about everything.

202
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
You've got amazing scenery like the quartzite mountains,

203
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
You've got a river that actually is doable.

204
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
It's not a dangerous river,

205
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
It's a very doable river, but it's a lot of fun.

206
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
And then you've got the big, huge white pine

207
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
and Tomoghani. you get a palace through

208
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
And it's fantastic.

209
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And just make sure you go with a good person.

210
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Social dynamics is more important on a trip.

211
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
You guys know that more than I do.

212
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
On the trip you just described with somebody

213
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
you didn't get along with very well

214
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and you wouldn't really be looking at the quartzite mountains

215
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
all that much would you.

216
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Because here's the thing: when you're in the wilderness,

217
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
you are who you are.

218
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
There's no faking your character,

219
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
There's no facade.

220
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And if you don't like your boss, for example,

221
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
the best thing to do is take them on a canoe trip

222
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
in May, when the bugs are up,

223
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Because they can't fake themselves.

224
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
And I love that. I love that about wilderness.

225
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
I am who I am out there,

226
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
You know. but you back really bad.

227
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
You have to sort of immerse yourself with it

228
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and become you know, in one sense

229
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
living out there.

230
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
So the people you're with,

231
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
even if you go canoe-tripping with someone

232
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and you never meet them

233
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
for 40 years,

234
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
you finally meet them. you're still friends.

235
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Yeah, No, you're right about that

236
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
That's for sure,

237
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Is that route that you were talking about,

238
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
one of those old routes

239
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
that you've opened back up.

240
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
It's an old canoe route

